-- title:
--   Theorem 3.4 — mean-based monotonic optimal no-regret play cannot converge almost surely to the NE of a competitive game
-- statement:
--   Let $(G,H)$ be a competitive $2\times2$ game (condition (a) or (b) of Definition 2.6) and $(p^*,q^*)$ its Nash equilibrium. Suppose player 1 plays $P_t=f_t(\widehat Q_{t-1})$ and player 2 plays $Q_t=g_t(\widehat P_{t-1})$, where $f$ and $g$ are monotonic mean-based strategies (Definitions 2.4 and 2.5), each with an optimal uniform no-regret rate $(1/2,c)$ (Definition 2.3; player 1 for $G$, player 2 for $H$), and the realized actions $I_t\sim\mathrm{Ber}(P_t)$, $J_t\sim\mathrm{Ber}(Q_t)$ are drawn independently given the past on a probability space $(\Omega,\mathcal F,\mu)$. Then it is **not** the case that, almost surely,
--   $$
--   P_t\to p^*\quad\text{and}\quad Q_t\to q^*\qquad(t\to\infty).
--   $$
--
--   This is the main result of the paper (Theorem 3.4): with feedback given by realized actions, the standard optimal no-regret algorithms of online-mirror-descent type (e.g. multiplicative weights with step $1/\sqrt t$) cannot achieve last-iterate convergence to the equilibrium in games such as matching pennies, even though their time-averages converge.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The conclusion is about the mixed strategies generated by the same play: $P_t$ and $Q_t$ are the strategies evaluated on the realized history. Lean round $n$ is paper round $n+1$, with $\widehat Q_0:=0$.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: "optimal no-regret repeated game strategies"; "the NE (p∗, q∗)" → each strategy has a uniform no-regret rate (1/2, c) for some c (player 2 for Hᵀ); (p∗, q∗) any Nash equilibrium; reason: "optimal" is r = 1/2 in Definition 2.3 (pp.7, 10); a competitive game has exactly one NE (p.8), so "a NE" is "the NE"
--   - encoding: the repeated game with independent implementation of mixed strategies (pp.5–6, 18) → IsPlay: measurable actions and conditional law Ber(P_t) ⊗ Ber(Q_t) given σ((I_s, J_s)_{s<t}); reason: the model of §2 written with conditional expectations; any such space carries the same law
--   - encoding: Q̂_0 in round 1 undefined → Q̂_0 := 0; reason: only fixes the value f_1(0) played in round 1; changes no instance of the theorem
--   - encoding: uniform no-regret rate (r, c) for a finite c: lim sup_T max_J T^{−r} Reg_T(J) ≤ c → ∃ c, for all large T and all J, Reg_T(J) ≤ c·T^r; reason: equivalent, since the maximum over {0,1}^T is finite; avoids a real-valued limsup
--   - encoding: (p∗, q∗) a Nash equilibrium in mixed strategies; actions 0, 1 → published RankOneBimatrix.BinSearch.IsNE on the probability vectors (1 − p, p), (1 − q, q); actions Fin 2; reason: Lean convention and published vocabulary; same instances
--   - encoding: rounds t = 1, 2, …; f_t applied to (J)^{t−1} → Lean rounds n = t − 1 from 0; F n applied to the first n actions; reason: Lean convention; same instances
-- source:
--   Muthukumar, Phade, Sahai, On the Impossibility of Convergence of Mixed Strategies with Optimal No-Regret Learning, arXiv:2012.02125v3, Theorem 3.4, p.16 (proof pp.17–20)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_RankOneBimatrix_BinSearch_Game
import Definitions.Def_NoRegretLastIterate_Game
import Definitions.Def_NoRegretLastIterate_Strategy
import Definitions.Def_NoRegretLastIterate_MeanBased
import Definitions.Def_NoRegretLastIterate_Play

open MeasureTheory Matrix Filter Topology

namespace NoRegretLastIterate

/-- Muthukumar, Phade, Sahai, arXiv:2012.02125v3, Theorem 3.4, p.16: "If both players 1 and 2 use
optimal no-regret repeated game strategies that are mean-based (Definition 2.4) and monotonic
(Definition 2.5), then the pair of mixed strategies of both the players (P_t, Q_t) cannot converge
to the NE (p∗, q∗) almost surely." For every competitive game `(G, H)` (condition (a) or (b)) with
Nash equilibrium `(p∗, q∗)`, every pair of monotonic mean-based strategies `f`, `g`, each with an
optimal no-regret rate `(1/2, c)` (player 1 for `G`, player 2 for `H`), and every probability
space `(Ω, μ, I, J)` carrying their realization-based play, it is not the case that almost surely
`P_t → p∗` and `Q_t → q∗`, where `P_t = f_t(Q̂_{t−1})` and `Q_t = g_t(P̂_{t−1})`.

**Formalization Note** Lean round `n` is paper round `n + 1`; `meanBased f n (J^n)` is
`f_{n+1}(Q̂_n)`, with the empty average `Q̂_0 := 0`. Player 2's regret is the row-player regret of
`Hᵀ`. Nash equilibrium is the published `RankOneBimatrix.BinSearch.IsNE` on `(1 − p, p)`,
`(1 − q, q)`; competitive games have exactly one (p.8), so "a NE" is "the NE". -/
theorem not_ae_tendsto_nash {G H : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℝ} (hcomp : IsCompetitive G H)
    {p q : ℝ} (hNE : RankOneBimatrix.BinSearch.IsNE G H (mix p) (mix q))
    {f g : ℕ → ℝ → ℝ} (hf : IsMonotonicMeanBased f) (hg : IsMonotonicMeanBased g)
    (hfr : HasRate G (meanBased f) (1 / 2)) (hgr : HasRate Hᵀ (meanBased g) (1 / 2))
    {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (μ : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ]
    (I J : ℕ → Ω → Fin 2) (hplay : IsPlay μ (meanBased f) (meanBased g) I J) :
    ¬ (∀ᵐ ω ∂μ, Tendsto (fun n => meanBased f n (fun k : Fin n => J k ω)) atTop (𝓝 p) ∧
        Tendsto (fun n => meanBased g n (fun k : Fin n => I k ω)) atTop (𝓝 q)) := by sorry

end NoRegretLastIterate
